# Line endings.
#
# `text=auto` stores every text file with LF *in the repository* while leaving
# each machine's working copy alone. That is what keeps a Windows checkout and
# a Linux CI runner from seeing every file as a whole-file diff.
#
# It deliberately does NOT say `eol=lf` here. That variant forces LF into the
# working tree too, which breaks the Windows batch files in this project - a
# .bat with LF endings misparses - and git refuses the conversion outright
# ("CRLF would be replaced by LF") rather than corrupt them silently. The
# per-type rules below set the working-tree ending each file format actually
# requires.
* text=auto

# Windows scripts: must keep CRLF in the working tree or cmd.exe mis-handles
# them. Still stored as LF in the repository.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts and Dockerfiles: must keep LF even when checked out on Windows,
# or the shell reads the trailing CR as part of the command.
*.sh         text eol=lf
Dockerfile   text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile text eol=lf

# Binary assets must never be line-ending converted.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.zip   binary

# Generated lockfiles: kept in the repository, but collapsed in diffs and
# excluded from the language statistics.
package-lock.json linguist-generated=true
